### HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS)

Even when a server supports HTTPS, users may still connect over plain HTTP - by typing `http://` explicitly, following an old link, or being silently downgraded by an attacker on the network. HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) is a response header that instructs clients to always use HTTPS for a domain, refusing plain HTTP connections entirely:

```
Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
```

When a client sees this header, it records that domain as requiring HTTPS for the duration specified by `max-age` (in seconds) and refuses to make plain HTTP connections for that period. This protects against **SSL stripping** attacks, where an attacker positioned between the client and server intercepts a plain HTTP connection and prevents it from being upgraded to HTTPS.

Check whether the web server currently sends an HSTS header:

```
# On: desktop
curl -kI https://<%= $webserver_ip %>
```

The `-I` flag fetches only the response headers. You will find that `Strict-Transport-Security` is not present - the web server is not yet enforcing HTTPS-only connections. We will revisit this in a later lab when we look at man-in-the-middle attacks, where SSL stripping is demonstrated in practice and HSTS is one of the defences you will apply.
